--TEST--
An `array<int>` or `array<uint>` that already exists is not an `array<float>`, at any depth, as an assignment, an argument, a return or a spread, because its elements are not converted where it is passed on; `as array<float>` is the conversion — `rule:types/arrays`, `rule:types/implicit-widening`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:types/array-t

class Shop
{
    public static function total(array<float> $xs): float
    {
        return 0.0;
    }

    public static function back(array<int> $xs): array<float>
    {
        return $xs;
    }
}

array<int> $whole = [1, 2];

// An assignment.
array<float> $f = $whole;

// An argument.
echo Shop::total($whole), "\n";

// A nullable target, and a nested array.
?array<float> $maybe = $whole;
array<array<int>> $rows = [[1]];
array<array<float>> $grid = $rows;

// A `uint` element type.
array<uint> $counts = [1];
array<float> $c = $counts;

// A spread into an array literal.
array<float> $spread = [...$whole];
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0403]: this method declares `array<float>` but returns `array<int>`
   --> case.nvs:13:16
%A
   = help: an `array<int>` that already exists keeps the type of its elements, so it is not converted to `array<float>`. Convert it with `as`, which builds a new array: `$value as array<float>`
%A
